    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A more in-depth understanding arises from viewing a quantum particle as a wave packet in momentum space. If we attempt to precisely define a particle's position, we must construct a wave packet that is highly localized, meaning it is a superposition of many different momentum eigenstates. According to Fourier transform principles, confining a wave packet sharply in real space ($\\Delta x$ small) necessitates including a broad range of wavelengths, which translates directly to a large uncertainty in the momentum ($\\Delta p$ large). This unavoidable mathematical consequence of representing quantum states as wave functions is the physical essence of the uncertainty principle. Furthermore, this principle extends to other complementary pairs, such as energy ($\\Delta E$) and time ($\\Delta t$), yielding $\\Delta E \\Delta t \\geq \\hbar/2$, which governs the minimum lifetime of excited quantum states.",
