      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To significantly elaborate, consider the wave packet approach in detail: a localized position wave function, $\\psi(x)$, is mathematically represented as a superposition integral over momentum states, $\\phi(p)$, via the inverse Fourier transform. The width of this momentum representation, $\\Delta p$, dictates how rapidly the localizing wave function must decay in position space, $\\Delta x$. Because the Fourier transform inherently maps wide spectral content (many $p$ values) to confined spatial features, ensuring $\\psi(x)$ is sharply peaked requires integrating over a broad range of $p$, thus mandating a large $\\Delta p$. This contrasts sharply with classical intuition, where measurement errors are attributed solely to imperfect apparatus; in quantum mechanics, the uncertainty is inherent to the state itself. This necessity for a spread-out momentum profile to achieve spatial resolution demonstrates that position and momentum are not independent variables but are fundamentally linked through the mathematical structure of quantum mechanics, specifically the constraints imposed by the Fourier pair relationship.",
